<!--
  IL2CPP STRIPPING EXCEPTIONS.

  (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment, and no hyphen at the end of a
  line. XML forbids both inside a comment, and UnityLinker does not soften the
  blow: it dies with "Fatal error in Unity CIL Linker / System.Xml.XmlException"
  fifteen seconds into the build, which reads like a broken toolchain rather
  than like a typo in a rule file.)

  System.Threading.OSSpecificSynchronizationContext

  2026-09-06. Every WebGL build from a COLD sandbox died in il2cpp with

      Error: IL2CPP error (no further information about what managed code
      was being converted is available)
      System.Collections.Generic.KeyNotFoundException: The given key
      'System.Void System.Threading.OSSpecificSynchronizationContext::.ctor(
      System.Object)' was not present in the dictionary.
        at Unity.IL2CPP.Metadata.MetadataCollector.GetMethodIndex(...)
        at Unity.IL2CPP.Metadata.Dat.SectionWriters.TypeDefinitionsWriter...

  The managed stripper keeps the TYPE and drops its constructor, and the
  metadata writer then asks for a method index that was never registered.
  Preserving the type keeps the two in step.

  IT DOES NOT LOOK LIKE THIS WHEN YOU MEET IT, which is the reason for this
  comment. il2cpp leaves a PARTIAL Library/Bee/artifacts/WebGL/il2cppOutput
  behind when it throws, and the incremental build trusts it, so the next
  three attempts failed in three different tools and none of them named the
  real cause:

      wasm-opt   crashed outright, 0xC0000374 (STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION)
      wasm-opt   [parse exception: invalid heap type index: 524295]
      clang      "Building ...\uvr7rbqt5rl1.o failed with output:" and then
                 nothing at all

  It is the same shape the build workflow notes already record for a killed
  build: one bad input, a new error per fix attempt. Clearing artifacts one
  layer at a time only moves the symptom. Only a build with Library/Bee AND
  Library/Il2cppBuildCache both gone showed the exception underneath, and it
  reproduced identically on a clean checkout, so this is not anyone's code.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="mscorlib">
    <type fullname="System.Threading.OSSpecificSynchronizationContext" preserve="all" />
  </assembly>
</linker>
